        "content": "**Step 3: find the new swing high after a break down**\nPrice breaks below the swing low. Now find the highest point between the lower low and the new breakdown. That is your new swing high. The old range is no longer valid, so you have a new range. You have a swing high, but not yet a swing low.\n\n**Step 4: find the swing low**\nWait for a three-candle formation where one candle is clearly lower than the others. That is only a potential swing low, not confirmed. Draw the red line at the highest point of that candle. When price closes above that line, the swing low is validated.\n\nThe host's reaction: *\"I never thought about putting the line there. That is a brilliant idea.\"*\n\n**Step 5: mark the liquidity and the new range**\nNow you have a swing low and a swing high. The swing low is the sell-side liquidity and the swing high is the buy-side liquidity. A lot of liquidity now lies above and below. This is the new range, the area you are working with. Wait until price breaks out beyond these liquidity levels. Until then do not mark new swings inside the range."
