    "summary": "Walkthrough of market reversals, breakout scenarios, and the recurring trading cycle.",
    "content": "These last examples repeat the same rules in new situations. Read each as a small test of the rules.\n\n- **Example A: nothing to do**: No breaks and no body closes happened. There are only a few liquidity sweeps. A sweep is not a swing and not a breakout, so you ignore it for now.\n- **Example B: a body-close breakout**: A breakout happens with a body close. But the new high is not yet a swing, because no candle has formed next to it. You cannot say it is a swing yet. Then the next candle comes and the swing high is confirmed. The range changes to the new one.\n- **Example C: the bearish structure breaks (a reversal)**: The host notices this is a reversal of the pattern: the bearish structure is being broken. One candidate high cannot be called a swing high, because price had not gone below the area first. Skip it and wait for a nice candle. The next high is validated and becomes the new swing high. Now find the new swing low: look for the minimum point between the two swing highs. That is the new swing low, and you simply continue like that.\n\n### Pattern to Remember for the Whole Walkthrough\n1. Mark the range (last structural high and low).\n2. Do nothing inside the range.\n3. When price breaks out (body close), find the extreme point between the swings.\n4. Wait for the three candles and the red-line confirmation.\n5. Mark the new range and repeat.",
